  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively establish several relevant general principles: casting or rapid cooling can preserve metastable phase constitutions; composition and thermal history can control multiphase HEA structures; C14 Laves phases can coexist with solid-solution matrices; and magnetic constituents or spin fluctuations can affect phase stability and magnetic behavior. R9 and R20 are especially relevant to rapid-cooling stabilization, while R2, R6, R11, and R19 support Laves-containing multiphase structures, and R10 and R12 provide limited magnetic context. However, none describes FeCoNiTi, an fcc/C14 combination produced by rapid arc-melt quenching, a compositionally matched C14-dominant comparator, antiferromagnetic coupling between Fe/Co/Ni and Ti sublattices, or C14-assisted orbital/spin-canted domain-wall pinning leading to roughly 667 Oe coercivity at 400 K. Thus the literature points broadly toward phase-control and magnetic-structure concepts, but reconstructing the specific intervention and mechanism would require a substantial new synthesis."
}
